  eyebrow: 'A sundial shaped for the place you live',
  lede: 'An ordinary sundial is faithful to the sun and, for that very reason, wrong to the clock: by up to a quarter of an hour, depending on the season. The Earth travels an ellipse and leans as it goes, so the sun itself arrives early in autumn and late in winter. In 1892 a British general, and in 1966 a watchmaker from the Black Forest, found the same quiet remedy. Give the shadow-maker a waist that changes with the date, and let the edge of its shadow carry the correction. This page shapes such a dial for your own sky, ready to print.',
