  chip1: 'Spring equinox, noon', chip2: 'Midsummer, half past seven', chip3: '3 November, the sun 16 minutes early', chip4: 'Winter solstice, noon',
  ch1n: 'One', ch1h: 'Why the sun runs early and late',
  ch1p1: 'A sundial reads the sun’s position, and the sun does not keep a steady pace. In January the Earth is nearest the sun and hurries along its path; in July it lingers. The lean of the axis stretches and squeezes the sun’s daily progress a second time. Add the two and you get the equation of time: the sun is up to sixteen minutes early in early November and fourteen minutes late in mid-February. Only four days a year does it agree with the clock.',
  ch1p2: 'Astronomers draw this drift as a figure of eight, the analemma. The one beside these words is computed for your place and year from the same formulas that shape the roller.',
  ch1cap: 'Analemma at {lat}, {lon}: the sun’s lead against its height, one dot per month',
  ch2n: 'Two', ch2h: 'A waist that knows the date',
  ch2p1: 'John Ryder Oliver, a major-general, patented the idea in London in 1892. Martin Bernhardt, a watchmaker from Freudenstadt, worked it out again from memory during long years of captivity after the war, and built the first one in 1966 for a competition of the magazine Sky & Telescope. About one hundred and fifty of his dials keep time in public squares today.',
  ch2p2: 'The trick is simple to say and lovely to watch. A roller throws a broad shadow with two edges; you read the leading one. As the seasons carry the sun higher and lower, a different part of the roller does the casting, and there it is exactly as wide as that day’s correction asks. The correction is not written on the dial. It is carved into the gnomon.',
  ch2quote: 'Every height of the sun returns twice a year with a different correction, so there are two rollers: one for the rising half of the year, one for the falling half, exchanged at the solstices.',
  ch2capEmpty: 'compute a dial to see its two rollers',
  ch2cap: 'Your two rollers, to scale: {rmin} to {rmax} mm in radius over {len} mm',
  ch3n: 'Three', ch3h: 'A shape the light draws',
  ch3p1: 'The crescent is not decoration. The plate is a negotiation with the low winter sun. Where the sun must reach the scale from above, the plate sinks into a dish. Where the early and late sun must pass across to the far side, the wing thins into a blade in the plane of the scale, so that only for a few days around the equinoxes does its shadow get in the way. Wherever neither is possible, the plate is cut away, and that cut is the inner edge of the crescent.',
  ch3p2: 'The generator traces every sunlit quarter hour of the winter half-years across the dial and lets those rays decide. Move the latitude and the dish deepens, the wings twist, the horns reach for the summer hours.',
  ch4n: 'Four', ch4h: 'Make yours',
  ch4p: 'Choose the place where it will stand. The zone offset is looked up for you; the dial shows standard time all year and carries a small engraved reminder to add an hour in summer.',
